.TH HOG 1 2026-09-12 hog "hog manual"
.SH NAME
hog \- read JSON logs line by line and print them for humans
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBhog\fR [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-\-exclude\fR] [\fB\-E\fR|\fB\-\-reset\-exclude\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-ts\-field\fR] [\fB\-\-ts\-format\fR] [\fB\-\-level\-field\fR] [\fB\-\-msg\-field\fR] [\fB\-\-timezone\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIARGS\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
hog reads a stream of JSON log lines and prints each one as a single readable
line: a timestamp, a level tag, the message, and the remaining fields as
key=value pairs. Nested objects are flattened into dotted keys, so
{"grpc":{"code":"OK"}} prints as grpc.code=OK. A line that does not start with
{ is passed through byte for byte, which keeps stack traces, build banners and
ssh diagnostics intact.
.PP
hog reads from one of two places. With no positional arguments it reads standard
input, so `tail \-f app.log | hog` and `hog < app.log` work as expected. With
positional arguments it runs the command template from its config file,
substituting the arguments into it by position, and renders that command\*(Aqs
standard output. The template runs without a local shell.
.PP
Nothing is hidden from the output unless a config file or \-e says so, and the
key order in the tail is alphabetical by default so that two lines can be
compared column by column.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-exclude\fR \fI<FIELD>\fR
Hide FIELD and everything under it; repeatable and comma\-separated.

Paths are dotted and matched on segment boundaries: `\-e grpc` hides `grpc.code`, but never `grpcStatus`. Adds to the config\*(Aqs list.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR, \fB\-\-reset\-exclude\fR
Ignore the exclude list from the config file.

Combine with `\-e` to replace it outright: `hog \-E \-e foo`.
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR
When to colourise output; the default is `auto`.

`NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured, and the config file\*(Aqs `output.color` sets it when this flag is absent.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Print the command that would run, with the arguments substituted, and exit without running it
.TP
\fB\-\-ts\-field\fR \fI<FIELD>\fR
JSON field holding the timestamp; replaces the candidate list
.TP
\fB\-\-ts\-format\fR \fI<FMT>\fR
Timestamp output format: a strftime pattern, or `raw`, or `none`
.TP
\fB\-\-level\-field\fR \fI<FIELD>\fR
JSON field holding the level; replaces the candidate list
.TP
\fB\-\-msg\-field\fR \fI<FIELD>\fR
JSON field holding the message; replaces the candidate list
.TP
\fB\-\-timezone\fR \fI<TZ>\fR
Time zone for the timestamp column: `local`, `utc`, or an IANA name
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Path to the config file (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hog/config.toml)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the release tag this binary was built from.

`\-V` is accepted as well: it is clap\*(Aqs standard short form and people have the muscle memory. There is no `\-\-verbose` in hog to collide with.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
[\fIARGS\fR]
Substituted into the config\*(Aqs `command` template by position: `{0}` is the first, `{1}` the second, and so on.

With no arguments `hog` reads stdin instead. An argument spelled like a subcommand is passed after `\-\-`: `hog \-\- config api`.
.SH COMMANDS
A subcommand name is matched before the first positional argument, so an argument that is spelled like one is passed after \-\-: `hog \-\- config api`.
.TP
\fBhog config\fR
Inspect and edit the config file
.TP
\fBhog config command\fR
Show the command template, or change it
.TP
\fBhog config command set\fR \fI<TEMPLATE>\fR
Write a new command template, after checking that it works
.TP
\fBhog config edit\fR
Open the config file in $VISUAL or $EDITOR
.TP
\fBhog config exclude\fR
Show the persistent exclude list, or change it
.TP
\fBhog config exclude add\fR \fI<FIELD>...\fR
Append FIELDs to the exclude list, keeping every comment in the file
.TP
\fBhog config exclude rm\fR \fI<FIELD>...\fR
Remove FIELDs from the exclude list
.TP
\fBhog config init\fR
Write a commented starter config if none exists
.TP
\fBhog config path\fR
Print the path of the config file that is actually read
.SH "INPUT MODES"
The mode is chosen by the presence of positional arguments. There is no flag for it, and no other heuristic.
.TP
\fBarguments given\fR
The config file\*(Aqs command template runs, with the arguments substituted into it. Standard input is not read. If the config sets no template, the built\-in `echo {@}` runs, so a brand\-new install shows where its arguments go.
.TP
\fBno arguments, standard input is a pipe or a file\fR
Standard input is rendered.
.TP
\fBno arguments, standard input is a terminal\fR
The configured command runs. With no configured command hog prints its help and exits 2, rather than waiting forever for JSON to be typed at it.
.PP
\-\-dry\-run always takes the command branch: it answers `what would you run?`, which is not a question about standard input.
.SH "COMMAND TEMPLATE"
The `command` key in the config file is a command line. hog splits it into argv by shell quoting rules and executes it directly: there is no local shell, so a redirection or a pipe written outside quotes becomes a literal argument. Inside quotes it is the remote shell\*(Aqs business, and works.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
command = "ssh \-tt \-o ServerAliveInterval=15 {0} \\
             \*(Aqdocker logs \-f \-\-since 1h bpam\-{1}\-1\*(Aq"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Placeholders are substituted into the split words:
.TP
\fB{0}, {1}, ...\fR
The first, second, ... positional argument. Indices must run from {0} with no gaps: a template using {0} and {2} is rejected when the config is read.
.TP
\fB{@}\fR
Every argument no index took. With N the highest index used, {@} is arguments N+1 onwards; with no indexed placeholder it is all of them. It expands to separate argv words in place, and to nothing at all when there are none, like "$@" in a shell. It must be a whole word of its own: a second {@}, or a template writing bpam\-{@}\-1, is rejected.
.TP
\fB{}, {{0}}, {{@}}\fR
Literal. A bare {} is left alone so that `find \-exec {} \\;` and `docker \-\-format \*(Aq{{.Names}}\*(Aq` survive; {{0}} produces a literal {0}.
.PP
Arity is checked. A template with {0} and {1} needs exactly two arguments; with {@} it accepts any number; with both it has a floor and no ceiling. `hog \-\-help` prints the configured template, its arity and an example.
.PP
Arguments are validated before substitution, never quoted. hog cannot know whether an argument lands in a word the remote shell will split again, as in \*(Aqdocker logs bpam\-{1}\-1\*(Aq, or in a bare argv entry, as in \-l app={1}, where it would not. Quoting is therefore not solvable in the general case, and hog does not try. Instead only [A\-Za\-z0\-9._:/@\-] is accepted, at most 256 bytes per argument. That covers docker, Kubernetes and systemd names, user@host and host:port, and rejects every character that could break out of a word at either level.
.PP
An argument beginning with \- passes that whitelist by construction, and reaches the spawned program\*(Aqs argv as a flag of that program. See NOTES.
.SH "EXCLUDING FIELDS"
An exclusion is a dotted path, matched on segment boundaries, and it prunes the whole subtree under it. `grpc` hides grpc.code and grpc.request.deadline; it does not hide grpcStatus, which is a different key that merely starts with the same letters. That is why exclusions are dotted paths rather than globs or substrings: a glob such as grpc.* invites exactly the grpcStatus mistake, while subtree pruning already covers the case it was wanted for.
.TP
\fBhog\fR
The list from the config file.
.TP
\fBhog \-e foo\fR
The list from the config file, plus foo.
.TP
\fBhog \-E\fR
Nothing is hidden.
.TP
\fBhog \-E \-e foo\fR
Exactly foo; this is how the file\*(Aqs list is replaced.
.PP
\-e is repeatable and comma\-separated: `\-e a,b \-e c` excludes a, b and c. `hog config exclude add` and `hog config exclude rm` change the persistent list in the file, keeping its comments.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The first of these that exists wins; the files are never merged. Values layer as built\-in defaults, then the config file, then command\-line flags.
.TP
\fB\-\-config PATH\fR
Used as given. A missing file is an error.
.TP
\fB$HOG_CONFIG\fR
The same, from the environment.
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hog/config.toml\fR
Or $HOME/.config/hog/config.toml when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is unset. This holds on macOS too: hog is a developer CLI that belongs in dotfiles next to ~/.config/gh, and the same file has to work unchanged on the Linux hosts it logs into.
.PP
There is no implicit ./hog.toml and no walk up the directory tree. The config names a command that hog executes, so picking one up from the current directory would make `git clone && cd && hog prod api` a way to run someone else\*(Aqs command. Use \-\-config ./hog.toml when that is what is wanted.
.PP
`hog config init` writes a commented starter file. An unknown key is a warning naming the line, never an error, so a config written for a newer hog still runs here.
.TP
\fBexclude\fR
Array of dotted paths to hide. Top\-level key.
.TP
\fBcommand\fR
The command template. Top\-level key.
.TP
\fB[fields] ts, level, msg\fR
Candidate key names for the three columns; the first one present in a line wins, decided per line.
.TP
\fB[output] time_format\fR
A jiff strftime pattern such as %H:%M:%S, or `raw` to print the timestamp byte for byte, or `none` to drop the column.
.TP
\fB[output] time_zone\fR
`local` (default), `utc`, or an IANA name such as Europe/Moscow.
.TP
\fB[output] color\fR
`auto`, `always` or `never`.
.TP
\fB[output] sort_keys\fR
true sorts the tail alphabetically; false keeps the JSON order.
.TP
\fB[output] key_colors\fR
Hex colours for key names. A key\*(Aqs colour is FNV\-1a(name) modulo the palette length, so it is stable across runs and machines.
.TP
\fB[output.levels]\fR
Maps a producer\*(Aqs own level spellings onto the ones hog knows, e.g. "30" = "info" for pino and bunyan.
.PP
command and exclude are top\-level keys and must appear above the first [table] header: a bare key written after [output] would be read as output.command.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
Success; the input stream ended.
.TP
\fB1\fR
Runtime error: the config, the template, or a rejected argument.
.TP
\fB2\fR
Usage error, including a bare hog on a terminal with nothing configured.
.TP
\fB127\fR
The template\*(Aqs first word is not an executable in PATH.
.TP
\fB141\fR
hog\*(Aqs own standard output was closed, as by `| head`.
.TP
\fBn\fR
Otherwise, the exit status of the command hog ran. A command killed by a signal reports 128 plus the signal number, as a shell would.
.PP
A command that fails also prints one line to standard error naming the status and how many lines had already been rendered, so that an ssh connection dropping after 1423 lines does not read like the log ending.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\fBHOG_CONFIG\fR
Config file to read, unless \-\-config is given.
.TP
\fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME, HOME\fR
Where the default config file is looked for.
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
Set to any value to disable colour.
.TP
\fBCLICOLOR_FORCE\fR
Set to any value to force colour on, even when not writing to a terminal.
.TP
\fBTERM\fR
Consulted to decide how much colour the terminal can take. True\-colour palettes are downgraded to 256 colours where needed.
.TP
\fBVISUAL, EDITOR\fR
The editor `hog config edit` launches, VISUAL first.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/hog/config.toml\fR
The config file. `hog config path` prints the one that is actually read.
.SH EXAMPLES
Render a file, hiding two noisy fields:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hog \-e trace_id,serviceName < app.log
.fi
.RE
.PP
Follow a local service:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
journalctl \-u api \-f \-o cat | hog
.fi
.RE
.PP
Store a template, then use it:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hog config command set "ssh \-tt \-o ServerAliveInterval=15 {0} \\
  \*(Aqdocker logs \-f \-\-since 1h bpam\-{1}\-1\*(Aq"
hog prod api
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what that would run, without connecting:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hog \-\-dry\-run prod api
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pass a variable tail through to the far end. The \-\- is needed because hog\*(Aqs own flags still parse after the positional arguments, so an unknown \-\-since would be a usage error rather than a value:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hog config command set "ssh \-tt {0} \*(Aqdocker logs \-f {@}\*(Aq"
hog prod \-\- \-\-since 30m \-\-tail 200
.fi
.RE
.SH "SHELL COMPLETIONS"
`hog completions SHELL` writes a completion script to standard output. SHELL is one of bash, elvish, fish, powershell or zsh. The command is hidden from \-\-help because it is run once at install time, not day to day.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
hog completions zsh  > "${fpath[1]}/_hog"
hog completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/hog
hog completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/hog.fish
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
.TP
\fBArguments that begin with a dash\fR
\- is on the argument whitelist, because host names and image tags contain it. An argument such as \-\-tail therefore passes validation and arrives in the spawned program\*(Aqs argv as a flag of that program, not of hog. There is no privilege escalation in this: no shell is involved, the argument stays one argv word, and the program was already named by your own config file. But the flags a template\*(Aqs program accepts are part of what a caller can reach, so a template should not be pointed at a program whose flags you would not want supplied. Pass such an argument after \-\-, since hog\*(Aqs own parser would otherwise read it as a hog flag: `hog prod \-\- \-\-tail`.
.TP
\fB\-tt and ServerAliveInterval in the starter template\fR
Both are load\-bearing, and both are easy to delete as noise. \-tt gives the remote command a pty: without one, a container that is quiet produces no traffic, ssh never sees EPIPE after hog exits, and `docker logs \-f` keeps running on the remote host after every Ctrl\-C. \-o ServerAliveInterval=15 replaces ssh\*(Aqs default of 0: without it a VPN that drops leaves hog waiting forever with no output and no error.
.TP
\fBMigrating from hulog\fR
hog prints the timestamp in the machine\*(Aqs time zone, because time_zone defaults to `local`. hulog kept the offset written in the input string, so a log stamped with Z printed as UTC wherever it was read. The rendered digits will therefore differ. Set time_zone = "utc" in [output], or pass \-\-timezone utc, to get hulog\*(Aqs numbers back.
.SH VERSION
`hog \-\-version` prints the release tag the binary was built from, or `dev` with the commit and a dirty marker for a local build.
